J. B. Jackson prize

H.H. Douglas Award

John Allais Jakle (born May 16, 1939) [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-:0-1]  is an American geographer. He is emeritus professor in the departments of geography and landscape architecture at the  [LINK: (University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Illinois_Urbana-Champaign] . [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-2]


# EARLY LIFE AND EDUCATION #

Jakle was born in 1939 in  [LINK: (Terre Haute, Indiana) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terre_Haute,_Indiana] . [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-3]  He grew up in  [LINK: (Michigan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michigan] . He graduated from  [LINK: (Culver Military Academy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culver_Military_Academy] . [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-4]

In 1961, Jakle graduated from  [LINK: (Western Michigan University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Michigan_University]  where he majored in geography and marketing. [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-5]  He received an MA from  [LINK: (Southern Illinois University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Illinois_University]  in 1963 and a PhD from  [LINK: (Indiana University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indiana_University]  in 1967. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-6]


# CAREER #

From 1965 to 1966, Jakle taught at the  [LINK: (University of Maine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Maine] . From 1966 to 1967, he taught at  [LINK: (Western Michigan University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Michigan_University] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-:0-1]  He then joined the geography faculty at the  [LINK: (University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Illinois_Urbana-Champaign] . He was the department head from  1990 to 1994. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-7]  He became professor emeritus in 2002. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-:0-1]

Since 1996, Jakle has partnered with historic preservationist Keith Sculle, who worked for the Illinois Historic Preservation Agency, [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-8]  on nine books on the material culture of the American automobile. [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-9]  The "roadside America" books they have written include titles on gas stations, fast-food restaurants, motels, road signs, and parking lots. [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-10]

Jake served on the committee which advocated for the Ohio River to be declared a National Heritage Corridor. [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-11]


# AWARDS AND HONORS #

In 2001, Jakle was awarded the J.B Jackson prize of the  [LINK: (American Association of Geographers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Association_of_Geographers]  for his book City Lights. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-12]

In 2004, Jakle received the H.H. Douglas Award of the  [LINK: (International Society for Landscapes, Place, & Material Culture) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=International_Society_for_Landscapes,_Place,_%26_Material_Culture&action=edit&redlink=1] . [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-13]


# PERSONAL LIFE #

In 1958, Jakle married Cynthia Powell; they have two daughters. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_A._Jakle#cite_note-:0-1]


